Chapter: SkySpec
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Lens
  cites Safari chapter Sky
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 Sky: how far dusk has come, how red the afterglow is, and the two packed colours
 that fall out of both.

 THE COLOURS ARE INTEGERS AND THAT IS WHY THIS SPEC IS SHAPED AS IT IS. The dusk
 fraction and the warmth are quotients of two-digit constants and land on
 sixteen-digit doubles, so grading them as reals would say more about the
 literals than about the sky. But `pack` rounds each channel and shifts it into
 one word, so the OUTPUT is exact -- and it is the output the blitter gets. The
 colour ladder below therefore grades seven steps of the route at 0.0, as
 integers, and the reals are graded only where they are exactly 0 or exactly 1.

 THAT IS NOT A WEAKER TEST THAN GRADING THE FRACTION. Both colours are a rounded
 mix, so a wrong fraction moves a channel and the packed word changes; the whole
 ramp is visible through them. What it does not pin is a fraction that is wrong by
 less than half a channel, which is a quarter of one per cent of the ramp and is
 not a defect anyone can act on.

 THE TWO COLOURS DIVERGE ONLY WHERE THERE IS WARMTH, and that is the assertion
 the ladder carries: at steps 0 to 2000 the sun is far above the horizon and the
 sky and the horizon are the SAME word; at 3000 to 5000 the disc is crossing and
 they differ; by 6000 it is deep below and they agree again. A chapter that mixed
 the red into the upper sky would fail the first and last of those.

 THE DARKENING IS SQUARED WHILE THE SUN IS UP AND LINEAR AFTER IT SETS, which is
 why the sky barely moves over the first two thousand steps and then falls away.
 The ladder is spaced evenly, so that shape reads straight off the numbers.

 We say:

Section: The Sun's Height

 Step 0 is the calibrated start and it is the one height that is exact. The disc
 is fully set when its top reaches the horizon, which is one radius below it.

  height-got : List Real
  height-got = [sun-height-px 0.0, sun-start-px, sun-radius-px, sun-fully-set-px, warmth-falloff-px, visible-bearing-limit]

  height-want : List Real
  height-want = [244.0, 244.0, 46.0, -46.0, 110.0, 1.4]

 At step 0 no dusk has happened and there is no warmth: the sun is 244 pixels up,
 which is more than two falloffs, so the warmth clamps at zero rather than going
 negative. Far down the route both have run to their stops.

  ends-got : List Real
  ends-got =
    [ sun-set-fraction 0.0, sunset-warmth 0.0,
      sun-set-fraction 6000.0, sunset-warmth 6000.0,
      sunset-glow ]

  ends-want : List Real
  ends-want = [0.0, 0.0, 1.0, 0.0, 0.85]

Section: The Colour Ladder

 Seven steps evenly spaced over the route. The sky word is the day-to-dusk mix
 alone; the horizon word takes that and mixes the red in at the warmth.

  sky-got : List Integer
  sky-got =
    [ sky-color 0.0, sky-color 1000.0, sky-color 2000.0, sky-color 3000.0,
      sky-color 4000.0, sky-color 5000.0, sky-color 6000.0 ]

  sky-want : List Integer
  sky-want = [9358054, 9160418, 8633304, 7710918, 6393260, 4219010, 2374238]

  horizon-got : List Integer
  horizon-got =
    [ horizon-color 0.0, horizon-color 1000.0, horizon-color 2000.0, horizon-color 3000.0,
      horizon-color 4000.0, horizon-color 5000.0, horizon-color 6000.0 ]

  horizon-want : List Integer
  horizon-want = [9358054, 9160418, 8633304, 10324366, 13262919, 8019301, 2374238]

 The three named colours pack to themselves, which is what says `pack` is a
 shift-and-or and not an arithmetic accident.

  packed-got : List Integer
  packed-got = [pack day-sky, pack dusk-sky, pack sunset-red]

  packed-want : List Integer
  packed-want = [9358054, 2374238, 14571572]

 A mix at either end is the end colour, and a mix at a half is the midpoint of
 each channel, rounded.

  mix-got : List Real
  mix-got =
    let a = lerp3 day-sky dusk-sky 0.0
    in let b = lerp3 day-sky dusk-sky 1.0
    in let m = lerp3 day-sky dusk-sky 0.5
    in [a.r, a.g, a.b, b.r, b.g, b.b, m.r, m.g, m.b]

  mix-want : List Real
  mix-want = [142.0, 202.0, 230.0, 36.0, 58.0, 94.0, 89.0, 130.0, 162.0]

Section: The Sun's Placement

 Off-heading by more than the limit and the sun is not on screen at all: the
 record answers False and three zeroes rather than an off-screen coordinate the
 blitter would have to check.

  facing : SunPos
  facing = sun-pos (0.0 - 2.2176) 0.0 focal 960.0

  away : SunPos
  away = sun-pos 0.0 0.0 focal 960.0

  visible-got : List Boolean
  visible-got = [facing.visible, away.visible]

  visible-want : List Boolean
  visible-want = [True, False]

 Looking straight at it, at the base focal: the disc is centred in the view and
 its height above the horizon is its pixel height, measured down from the middle
 of the screen. The scale is one because the focal is the base one.

  place-got : List Real
  place-got =
    [ facing.x, facing.y, facing.scale,
      away.x, away.y, away.scale ]

  place-want : List Real
  place-want = [480.0, 56.0, 1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "sk-height" height-got height-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "sk-ends  " ends-got ends-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "sk-sky   " sky-got sky-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "sk-horiz " horizon-got horizon-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "sk-packed" packed-got packed-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "sk-mix   " mix-got mix-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "sk-vis   " visible-got visible-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "sk-place " place-got place-want 0.0)
  end
